      "termination": "eos",
      "text": "Few fields make practical thinking so visible as mechanical engineering: an idea becomes a component, a system, and then something people can rely on every day. Ever since I was young, I have been fascinated by how machines work and motivated by the idea that thoughtful design can solve real-world problems. Mechanical engineering became the right path for me because it combines curiosity, creativity, and the drive to build systems that are efficient and reliable. Originally from Brazil, I have developed this interest through advanced study and applied project work, completing a Master\u2019s degree at a university ranked in the global top 500. That academic foundation strengthened my understanding of mechanical principles, design processes, and problem-solving, while also reinforcing the way I approach engineering work: translating complex mechanical concepts into practical, manufacturable solutions that improve performance without losing sight of cost, safety, or usability, so that design, production, and cross-functional teams can move effectively from concept through implementation.\n\nOne project with tangible outcomes was especially important in shaping my approach, because it required turning technical ideas into a result that could be delivered and used in practice rather than left at concept stage. Over a period of more than a year, I worked steadily with a tool and subject area that were new to me, moving from basic use to a point where I could apply them confidently in my work through regular practice and repeated application. In day-to-day engineering tasks, I am comfortable working through routine technical issues by following established procedures, and when an unusual case falls outside those steps, I make a point of seeking guidance so the work continues accurately and safely. I also work well alongside colleagues; for example, when teammates have asked how I approached a design step or used a tool, I have taken time to walk them through my process so they could apply it themselves.\n\nA personal reminder of why this profession matters came when I took my family to an open engineering showcase and my nephew became captivated by a prototype I helped design, excitedly explaining it to strangers as though he had built it himself. That moment stayed with me because it showed me that engineering does not only create products; it can also spark curiosity across generations. I am now eager to bring my academic background, practical mindset, and collaborative approach to a Mechanical Engineer role where I can continue developing while contributing positively to reliable, well-designed solutions.",
